raw:
{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "A police‑officer may arrest a person without a warrant when the person commits a cognizable offence in the officer’s presence, or when there is a reasonable complaint, credible information or reasonable suspicion that the person has committed a cognizable offence (whether punishable up to seven years or more) and the officer believes the arrest is necessary to prevent further offence, to investigate, to preserve evidence or to stop intimidation. The provision also lists other specific situations such as a person in possession of a house‑breaking implement, a proclaimed offender, someone found with suspected stolen property, anyone who obstructs a police‑officer, a deserter from the armed forces, a person involved in an act abroad that would be an offence in Bangladesh, a released convict breaching a rule, or when a requisition for arrest has been received from another officer. The officer may not arrest for the purpose of preventive detention.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "54",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "Any police-officer may, without an order from a Magistrate and without warrant, arrest- firstly, any person who commits, in the presence of a police-officer, a cognizable offence; secondly, any person against whom a reasonable complaint has been made, or credible information has been received, or a reasonable suspicion exists that he has committed a cognizable offence ... if the following conditions are satisfied, namely:- (i) the police-officer has reason to believe ... and (ii) the police-officer is satisfied that such arrest is necessary- (a) to prevent such person from committing any further offence; or (b) for proper investigation of the offence; or (c) to prevent such person from causing the evidence of the offence to disappear ..."
    },
    {
      "section": "54",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "thirdly, any person against whom credible information has been received that he has committed a cognizable offence punishable with imprisonment for a term which may extend to more than seven years whether with or without fine, or with death sentence ..."
    },
    {
      "section": "54",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "fourthly, any person having in his possession without lawful excuse, the burden of proving which excuse shall lie on such person, any implement of house breaking; fifthly, any person who has been proclaimed as an offender ... sixthly, any person in whose possession anything is found which may reasonably be suspected to be stolen property ..."
    },
    {
      "section": "54",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "seventhly, any person who obstructs a police-officer while in the execution of his duty ... eighthly, any person ... deserter from the armed forces of Bangladesh; ninthly, any person ... act ... out of Bangladesh, which, if committed in Bangladesh, would have been punishable as an offence ..."
    },
    {
      "section": "54",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "eleventhly, any person for whose arrest a requisition, has been received from another police-officer, provided that the requisition specifies the person to be arrested and the offence or other cause for which the arrest is to be made ..."
    },
    {
      "section": "54",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "(2) No police-officer shall arrest a person under this section for the purpose of detaining him under any law providing for preventive detention."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Police may detain a person arrested without a warrant for up to twenty‑four hours (excluding travel time to the Magistrate’s Court) unless a special order under section 167 is obtained. They must otherwise bring the arrested person before a Magistrate without unnecessary delay.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "61",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "shall not, in the absence of a special order of a Magistrate under section 167, exceed twenty-four hours exclusive of the time necessary for the journey from the place of arrest to the Magistrate's Court."
    },
    {
      "section": "60",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "shall, without unnecessary delay and subject to the provisions herein contained as to bail, take or send the person arrested before a Magistrate having jurisdiction in the case, or before the officer in charge of a police-station."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "The search of a woman must be carried out by another woman, observing strict decency.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "52",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "Whenever it is necessary to cause a woman to be searched, the search shall be made by another woman, with strict regard to decency."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Only the amount of restraint that is necessary to prevent the arrested person’s escape may be used; no more restraint than that is permitted.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "50",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "The person arrested shall not be subjected to more restraint than is necessary to prevent his escape."
    },
    {
      "section": "46",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "In making an arrest the police-officer or other person making the same shall actually touch or confine the body of the person to be arrested, unless there be a submission to the custody by word or action."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "When making an arrest a police officer must: \n1. Bear an accurate, visible and clear identification of his name. \n2. Disclose his identity and, if demanded, show his identity card to the arrested person and any witnesses. \n3. Prepare a memorandum of arrest that is attested by a suitable witness and have the arrested person countersign it. \n4. Inform a family member, relative or nominated friend of the arrest within 24 hours, giving the time, place of arrest and place of custody. \n5. If the arrested person has any injury, ensure medical examination and first‑aid, obtain a medical certificate and record the reasons for the injury. \n6. Allow the arrested person, if he wishes, to consult a lawyer of his choice or meet a close relative, preferably within twelve hours of the arrest.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "46A",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "(a) bear an accurate, visible and clear identification of his name which will facilitate easy identification;"
    },
    {
      "section": "46A",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "(b) disclose his identity and if demanded, shall show his identity card to the person arrested and to the persons present at the time of arrest;"
    },
    {
      "section": "46A",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "(c) prepare a memorandum of arrest which shall be- (i)attested by at least one witness, who is a member of the family of the person arrested or a respectable inhabitant of the locality where the arrest is made and where no such witness is available, the reasons thereof be recorded in the memorandum;"
    },
    {
      "section": "46A",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "(ii) countersigned or thumb-impressed by the person arrested if not refused by him;"
    },
    {
      "section": "46A",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "(d) intimate, where the accused is arrested from a place other than his residence, to a family member, relative, or a friend nominated by the person arrested, as soon as practicable but not later than twenty four hours from the time of such arrest, notifying the time and place of arrest and the place of custody;"
    },
    {
      "section": "46A",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "(e) ensure, if any mark of injury is found on the body of the person arrested, the examination and first aid treatment of the person by a medical officer or a registered medical practitioner, as the case may be, in accordance with section 46E; obtain a certificate from the attending medical officer or practitioner; and record the reasons for such injury;"
    },
    {
      "section": "46A",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "(f) allow the person arrested, if he so desires, to consult a lawyer of his choice or to meet any of his nearest relation, preferably within twelve hours of such arrest."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Yes. The Code of Criminal Procedure requires that a police officer inform the person being arrested of the reasons for the arrest at the time of the arrest.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "54A",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "Every police-officer arresting any person without warrant shall, at the time of making arrest, communicate to him the reasons for which he is arrested."
    },
    {
      "section": "54",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "the police-officer shall record while making such arrest, his reasons in writing"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "When information about a cognizable offence is given to the officer in charge of a police‑station, it must be reduced to writing (or written directly), read back to the informant, signed by the informant, and entered in a book kept by that officer in the form prescribed by the Government.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "154",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "Every information relating to the commission of a cognizable offence if given orally to an officer in charge of a police‑station, shall be reduced to writing by him or under his direction, and be read over to the informant; and every such information, whether given in writing or reduced to writing as aforesaid, shall be signed by the person giving it, and the substance thereof shall be entered in a book to be kept by such officer in such form as the Government may prescribe in this behalf."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "No. Under the Code of Criminal Procedure, a police officer may not investigate a non‑cognizable offence unless a Magistrate orders the investigation.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "155",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "No police-officer shall investigate a non-cognizable case without the order of a Magistrate of the first or second class having power to try such case"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Yes. Under the Code of Criminal Procedure an officer in charge of a police‑station may investigate a cognizable case without a Magistrate’s order.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "156",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "Any officer in charge of a police-station may, without the order of a Magistrate, investigate any cognizable case"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Yes. Under section 160 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, a police officer conducting an investigation may, by a written order, require the attendance of any person who appears to be acquainted with the circumstances of the case, and that person must attend as required.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "160",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "may, by order in writing, require the attendance before himself of any person ... who, from the information given or otherwise, appears to be acquainted with the circumstances of the case; and such person shall attend as so required."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "No. A person examined by a police officer is bound to answer all questions except those whose answers would tend to expose him to a criminal charge, penalty, or forfeiture.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "161",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "Such person shall be bound to answer all questions relating to such case put to him by such officer, other than questions the answers to which would have a tendency to expose him to a criminal charge or to a penalty or forfeiture."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "No. Under section 162 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, a statement made to a police officer, if reduced to writing, must not be signed by the person who made it.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "162",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "No statement made by any person to a police-officer in the course of an investigation ... shall, if reduced into writing, be signed by the person making it"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "No. A police officer is prohibited from offering any inducement, threat or promise to obtain a statement.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "163",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "No police-officer or other person in authority shall offer or make, or cause to be offered or made, any such inducement, threat or promise as is mentioned in the Evidence Act, 1872, section 24."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "A Magistrate who is a Metropolitan Magistrate, a Magistrate of the first class, or a Magistrate of the second class specially empowered by the Government (and who is not a police‑officer) may record a confession. Before doing so, the Magistrate must tell the person that he is not obliged to confess and that any confession he makes may be used as evidence against him.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "164",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "Any Metropolitan Magistrate, any Magistrate of the first class and any Magistrate of the second class specially empowered in this behalf by the Government may, if he is not a police‑officer record any statement or confession..."
    },
    {
      "section": "164",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "A Magistrate shall, before recording any such confession, explain to the person making it that he is not bound to make a confession and that if he does so it may be used as evidence against him..."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "If an investigation cannot be finished within the 24‑hour period, the police officer must immediately send a copy of the diary entries to the nearest Judicial Magistrate and forward the accused to that magistrate. The magistrate may then order a medical examination of the accused before he is handed over to police custody, and if any injury or claim of torture is shown, the magistrate must direct another medical examination. The magistrate can also authorize detention of the accused in police custody for up to fifteen days, or, if he lacks jurisdiction to try the case, order the accused to be sent to a magistrate who does have jurisdiction.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "167",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "shall forthwith transmit to the [nearest Judicial Magistrate] a copy of the entries in the diary hereinafter prescribed relating to the case, and shall at the same time forward the accused to such Magistrate."
    },
    {
      "section": "167",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "may order that the accused be examined by a medical officer of nearest government hospital before he is handed over to such custody; and upon expiry of the period of police custody, the accused shall be produced before the Magistrate without unnecessary delay; and if, upon such production, there appears to be any mark of injury on the body of the person accused, or the accused alleges that he has been subjected to torture during such custody, the Magistrate shall direct that the accused be examined by a medical officer of the nearest government hospital"
    },
    {
      "section": "167",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "may, whether he has or has not jurisdiction to try the case from time to time authorize the detention of the accused in such custody as such Magistrate thinks fit, for a term not exceeding fifteen days in the whole; and if he has not jurisdiction to try the case or send it for trial, and considers further detention unnecessary, he may order the accused to be forwarded to a Magistrate having such jurisdiction"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "A Magistrate may take cognizance of an offence (a) upon receiving a complaint of facts which constitute the offence; (b) upon a written report of such facts made by a police‑officer; or (c) upon information from any person other than a police‑officer, or on his own knowledge or suspicion that the offence has been committed.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "190",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "may take cognizance of any offence- (a) upon receiving a complaint of facts which constitute such offence; (b) upon a report in writing of such facts made by any police-officer; (c) upon information received from any person other than a police-officer, or upon his own knowledge or suspicion, that such offence has been committed."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "When a Magistrate takes cognizance of an offence on a complaint, he must immediately examine the complainant (and any witnesses present, if he considers it necessary) on oath, reduce the substance of that examination to writing, and have it signed by the complainant or witness and by the Magistrate. However, if the complaint is made in writing, the examination is not required, and likewise when the written complaint is made by a Court or a public servant acting in official duties.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "200",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "A Magistrate taking cognizance of an offence on complaint shall at once examine ... and the substance of the examination shall be reduced to writing and shall be signed ..."
    },
    {
      "section": "200",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "when the complaint is made in writing, nothing herein contained shall be deemed to require [such examination] ..."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "A Magistrate may postpone issuing process against the person complained about when, on receiving a complaint of an offence that he is authorised to take cognizance of (or that has been transferred to him under section 192), he thinks it appropriate and records his reasons in writing. This postponement is allowed to enable an inquiry or investigation to ascertain the truth or falsehood of the complaint, provided that, unless the complaint was made by a Court, the requirements of section 200 have been complied with, and even where the offence is triable exclusively by a Court of Session the Magistrate may still postpone the issue of process.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "202",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "Any Magistrate, on receipt of a complaint of an offence ... may, if he thinks fit, for reasons to be recorded in writing, postpone the issue of process for compelling the attendance of the person complained against ..."
    },
    {
      "section": "202",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "Provided that, save where the complaint has been made by a Court, no such direction shall be made unless the provisions of section 200 have been complied with."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "A magistrate may dismiss a complaint when, after considering the complainant’s statement on oath (if any) and the result of any investigation or inquiry, he judges that there is no sufficient ground for proceeding.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "203",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "there is in his judgment no sufficient ground for proceeding"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "An accused must be discharged before a charge is framed when (i) the Magistrate, after considering the record and hearing both sides, finds the charge to be groundless (section 241A); (ii) the Public Prosecutor withdraws the prosecution before a charge has been framed (section 494(a)); or (iii) after an inquiry under section 117 it is shown that no bond is required, the Magistrate discharges the person (section 119).",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "241A",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "if the Magistrate ... considers the charge to be groundless, he shall discharge the accused and record his reasons for so doing."
    },
    {
      "section": "494",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "if it is made before a charge has been framed, the accused shall be discharged in respect of such offence or offences"
    },
    {
      "section": "119",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "shall ... discharge him."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "If the complainant does not appear on the day fixed for the accused’s appearance (or any later adjourned date), the magistrate must acquit the accused, unless the magistrate decides it is appropriate to adjourn the hearing to another day.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "247",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "the Magistrate shall, notwithstanding anything herein before contained, acquit the accused, unless for some reason he thinks proper to adjoin the hearing of the case to some other day"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "When a magistrate is satisfied that an accusation was false and either frivolous or vexatious, he must record the reasons and may direct the complainant or informant to pay compensation to the accused (up to the prescribed maximum), may call the complainant to show cause why he should not pay, and if the complainant does not appear may issue a summons. The magistrate may also, in the same order, impose a default imprisonment of up to thirty days, and may further order imprisonment of up to six months or a fine of up to one lakh taka.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "250",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "if he is satisfied that the accusation was false and either frivolous or vexatious ... direct that compensation ... not exceeding [fifty thousand Taka] ... be paid by such complainant or informant to the accused"
    },
    {
      "section": "250",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "The Magistrate may, by the order directing payment of the compensation under sub-section (2), further order that, in default of payment, the person ordered to pay such compensation shall suffer simple imprisonment for a period not exceeding thirty days."
    },
    {
      "section": "250",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "the Magistrate may, in addition to the order directing payment of the compensation under sub-section (2), further order that the person ordered to pay such compensation shall also suffer imprisonment for a period not exceeding six months or pay a fine not exceeding [one lac taka]."
    },
    {
      "section": "250",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "call upon him forthwith to show cause why he should not pay compensation to such accused ... or, if such person is not present direct the issue of a summons to him to appear and show cause as aforesaid."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "A Metropolitan Magistrate or any Magistrate of the first class may tender a pardon to an accomplice. This power can be used in the case of (i) any offence triable exclusively by the Court of Session, (ii) any offence punishable with imprisonment which may extend to ten years, (iii) any offence punishable under section 211 of the Penal Code with imprisonment which may extend to seven years, or (iv) any offence under sections 216A, 369, 401, 435 or 477A of the Penal Code.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "337",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "In the case of any offence triable exclusively by the ... Court of Session, or any offence punishable with imprisonment which may extend to ten years, or any offence punishable under section 211 of the Penal Code, with imprisonment which may extend to seven years, or any offence under any of the following sections of the Penal Code, namely, sections 216A, 369, 401, 435 and 477A, [a Metropolitan Magistrate] or any Magistrate of the first class may ... tender a pardon to such person"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "An accused may be tried in his absence when the court is satisfied that he has absconded or is concealing himself and there is no immediate prospect of arrest, after the court has ordered him to appear and he fails to do so; also when, after having been produced or released on bail, the accused again absconds or fails to appear, the court may record its decision and try him in absentia; and when the judge or magistrate is satisfied, for recorded reasons, that the accused is incapable of remaining before the court and the accused is represented by an advocate, the court may dispense with his attendance and proceed with the trial in his absence. In cases where an accused has absconded and cannot be arrested, the court may also examine witnesses and record their depositions in his absence for use as evidence.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "339B",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "where the Court has reason to believe that an accused person has absconded or is concealing himself so that he cannot be arrested and produced for trial and there is no immediate prospect of arresting him ... if such person fails to comply with such direction, he shall be tried in his absence"
    },
    {
      "section": "339B",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "Where in a case after the production or appearance of an accused before the Court or his release on bail, the accused person absconds or fails to appear ... the Court ... shall, recording its decision so to do, try such person in his absence"
    },
    {
      "section": "540A",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "if the Judge or Magistrate is satisfied, for reasons to be recorded, that any one or more of [the] accused is or are incapable of remaining before the Court, he may, if such accused is represented by [an advocate], dispense with his attendance and proceed with such inquiry or trial in his absence"
    },
    {
      "section": "512",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "If it is proved that an accused person has absconded, and that there is no immediate prospect of arresting him, the Court ... may, in his absence, examine the witnesses ... and record their depositions"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Yes. Under the Code of Criminal Procedure a person who refuses to answer questions put to him by a criminal court may be punished. Section 485 allows the court to sentence such a person to simple imprisonment or to commit him to custody for up to seven days if he offers no reasonable excuse.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "485",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "If any witness or person ... refuses to answer such questions ... the Court may, for reasons to be recorded in writing, sentence him to simple imprisonment, or ... commit him to the custody of an officer of the Court for any term not exceeding seven days"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Yes. Under the Code of Criminal Procedure a criminal court is deemed an open court and the public generally may have access, subject to the judge’s power to restrict access when he thinks fit.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "352",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "The place in which any Criminal Court is held ... shall be deemed an open Court, to which the public generally may have access, so far as the same can conveniently contain them: Provided that the presiding Judge or Magistrate may, if he thinks fit, order ... that the public generally, or any particular person, shall not have access"
    },
    {
      "section": "12",
      "source": "PenalCode",
      "quote": "The word “public” includes any class of the public or any community."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "No. Under the Code of Criminal Procedure a person who has been acquitted cannot be tried again for the same offence while that acquittal remains in force.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "403",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "A person who has once been tried by a Court of competent jurisdiction for an offence and ... acquitted of such offence shall, while such conviction or acquittal remains in force, not be liable to be tried again for the same offence"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "An appeal against an order of acquittal may be presented either by the Government (by directing the Public Prosecutor to file the appeal) or by the complainant themselves, subject to the conditions in the statute.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "417",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "the Government may, in any case, direct the Public Prosecutor to present an appeal- (a) to the High Court Division from an original or appellate Order of acquittal passed by any Court of Session; (b) to the Court of Session from an original or appellate Order of acquittal passed by any Magistrate."
    },
    {
      "section": "417",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "the complainant may present an appeal- (a) to the High Court Division from an original order of acquittal passed by any Court of Session; (b) to the Court of Session from an original order of acquittal passed by any Magistrate."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "The High Court Division may, whenever it thinks fit, issue directions to (a) bring a person within its appellate criminal jurisdiction before the Court for legal disposition; (b) set at liberty a person who is illegally or improperly detained in public or private custody; (c) bring a prisoner detained in a jail within its limits before the Court to be examined as a witness; (d) bring a prisoner before a Court‑martial or any Commissioners for trial or examination of matters pending before them; and (e) remove a prisoner from one custody to another for the purpose of trial.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "491",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "that a person within the limits of its appellate criminal jurisdiction be brought up before the Court to be dealt with according to law; (b) that a person illegally or improperly detained in public or private custody with such limits be set at liberty; (c) that a prisoner detained in any jail situate within such limits be brought before the Court to be there examined as a witness; (d) that a prisoner detained as aforesaid be brought before a Court‑martial or any Commissioners for trial or to be examined touching any matter pending before such Court‑martial or Commissioners respectively; (e) that a prisoner within such limits be removed from one custody to another for the purpose of trial"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Yes. Under the Code of Criminal Procedure, a person who is not accused of a non‑bailable offence (i.e., accused of a bailable offence) and who is prepared to give bail must be released on bail, although the officer or court may instead accept a bond without sureties.",
  "citations": [
    {
      "section": "496",
      "source": "CrPC",
      "quote": "When any person other than a person accused of a non-bailable offence ... is prepared at any time while in the custody of such officer or at any stage of the proceedings before such Court to give bail, such person shall be released on bail"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "refused": false,
  "answer": "Bail may be granted to a person accused of a non‑bailable offence when he is arrested or detained without warrant, or when he appears before a Court, provided that there are no reasonable grounds for believing that he has been guilty of an offence punishable with death or transportation for life. The Court may also order bail for a person under sixteen years of age, any woman, or any sick or infirm accused of such an offence. Additionally, if the officer or Court finds that there are no reasonable grounds to believe the accused has committed a non‑bailable offence but there are sufficient grounds for further inquiry, the accused may be released on bail pending that inquiry.",
  "citations": [
    {
